Consequences

Security Posture

Cryptographic Binding: Stolen ciphertexts cannot be replayed or hijacked on unauthorized laptops because an attacker cannot sign the sync request without owning the corresponding private SSH key inside an active workspace.

Zero Credential Exposure: The raw LiteLLM key is never written to disk, and is only unsealed into the volatile, short-lived memory of the proxy cluster before forwarding to LiteLLM → AWS Bedrock.

Independence from Active Directory: No dedicated domain computer objects, Service Principal Names (SPNs), or AD Keytab mounts are required inside the EKS cluster.

Operations & Resilience

Airtight Crash Resilience: Developers experience zero session dropouts during proxy crashes, scaling, or rolling deployments. The X-Session-State cookie serves as the ultimate stateless recovery vector.

High-Performance Scaling: Rehydration checks are completely decentralised; any scaled proxy replica pod sharing the same SERVER_MASTER_KEY can decrypt the incoming state cookie and rehydrate its own local RAM cache inline on the fly.

VPC Self-Service Updates: If a developer's home IP shifts, they simply rerun the sync tool. The proxy seamlessly overwrites the old memory row and generates a fresh 24-hour cookie.
